## Roth conversion sizing (2026-08-19)

Task: add `maxConversionUnder(ceiling, ...)` to tax.ts plus a UI section that
picks a ceiling and reports the largest conversion that fits under it, the
resulting tax, and the marginal cost per dollar converted.

### tax.ts

Six ceilings, each with the income definition it actually caps — this is the
part that is easy to get wrong, because the six do NOT all constrain the same
number:

| ceiling                | measure                | single    | mfj       |
|------------------------|------------------------|-----------|-----------|
| top of 12% bracket     | ordinary taxable income| $48,475   | $96,950   |
| top of 22% bracket     | ordinary taxable income| $103,350  | $206,700  |
| SS 50% base            | provisional income     | $25,000   | $32,000   |
| SS 85% base            | provisional income     | $34,000   | $44,000   |
| top of 0% LTCG bracket | total taxable income   | $48,350   | $96,700   |
| IRMAA tier 1           | MAGI                   | $106,000  | $212,000  |

Only IRMAA needed a new constant (`IRMAA_TIER1_MAGI`); the other five are read
out of `FILING_PARAMS` / `LTCG_BRACKETS` (via a `bracketTop(fs, rate)` helper)
so they cannot drift from the charts. The full IRMAA tier table is still a
separate backlog item — tier 1 is all this feature needs.

`conversionMeasureValue(measure, ordinary, ss, ltcg, conversion, fs)` computes
each definition. The distinctions that matter:
- ordinary taxable income EXCLUDES ltcg (gains stack on top of the ordinary
  brackets), total taxable income includes it.
- MAGI = AGI + tax-exempt interest, and AGI already contains taxable SS. There
  is no muni input yet, so that term is zero for now.
- provisional income is the only measure that moves 1:1 with the conversion;
  the other three move faster, because a converted dollar also drags up to 85c
  of benefits in behind it.

Every measure is monotonically non-decreasing in the conversion, which is what
licenses the binary search in `maxConversionUnder`. It searches whole dollars
over [0, maxConversion=$1M], returns 0 with `alreadyOver` when the base
scenario already breaches the ceiling, and returns the bound with `unbounded`
when the ceiling is never reached. Float slack of 1e-6 on the comparison, since
the 0.5/0.85 inclusion factors put taxable income on fractions of a dollar.

`sizeConversion(...)` wraps it and prices the result via `totalTaxWithLTCG`
(which reduces to `totalTax` when ltcg is 0): taxBefore/taxAfter/taxCost,
`costPerDollar` (the average, i.e. "the marginal cost per dollar converted" the
backlog asked for), and `rateAboveCeiling`.

Gotcha worth remembering: `rateAboveCeiling` is sampled from conversion+1 to
conversion+2, NOT conversion to conversion+1. The dollar that straddles the
ceiling is split across two brackets and reads low — at the top of the 12%
bracket it came out as 14% (0.8 x 12% + 0.2 x 22%) rather than the 22% that
actually applies to the next several thousand dollars. The straddle exists
because ceilings are whole dollars while taxable income lands on fractions.

### The headline number

Default scenario (single, $30,000 ordinary, average $23,712 benefit, no gains),
ceiling = top of the 12% bracket:

  headroom $23,047 -> only $14,069 of conversion fits
  tax $2,813 -> $5,578, cost $2,765 = 19.65c per dollar converted
  rate past the ceiling: 22%

That 19.65% while nominally "in the 12% bracket" is the whole point of the
feature, and the headroom/conversion gap ($23,047 vs $14,069) is the torpedo
made concrete: each converted dollar drags 85c of benefits in behind it until
the 85% cap binds at $10,562 of conversion.

### UI

New emerald-accented section placed AFTER the Capital Gains Stacking section,
deliberately — it reuses that section's "Other Ordinary Income" slider, so the
input has to appear before the readout that depends on it. Inputs: a `<select>`
of the six ceilings (six is too many for the existing segmented control) and a
new "Long-Term Capital Gains You Plan to Realize" slider defaulting to $0.
The gains slider is what makes the 0%-LTCG and IRMAA ceilings mean anything.
Output: a five-tile `<dl className="stat-grid">` (largest conversion, tax after
with the before as a sub-note, extra tax, cost per dollar, rate past the
ceiling), then one of three prose paragraphs (normal / already-over /
unbounded), then the ceiling's own note.

CSS: `.section-heading-emerald`, `.slider-value.emerald`, `.slider-emerald`,
`.ceiling-select`, `.stat-grid`/`.stat`/`.stat-value`/`.stat-note`, following
the existing amber-accent pattern.

### Tests

+12, 65 -> 77 passing. tax.test.ts gets 8: the ceiling table wired to
FILING_PARAMS/LTCG_BRACKETS/IRMAA_TIER1_MAGI, the $14,069 headline with its
one-dollar boundary check, provisional ceilings landing exactly (25,000-11,856
= 13,144), gains crowding out the 0% LTCG ceiling, MAGI including capped
taxable SS, already-over, unbounded, the priced readout, and a property test
sweeping 6 ceilings x 2 filing statuses x 4 scenarios asserting the answer fits
and one more dollar does not. App.test.tsx gets 3 covering the default readout,
switching ceilings (incl. the already-over copy), and the gains slider.

Note for whoever writes the property test next: this project's vitest is typed
through @types/jest, so the `expect(value, message)` two-arg form does NOT
typecheck. Collect failures into a string[] and `expect(failures).toEqual([])`
instead — better diffs anyway.

Verified: `npx tsc -b`, `npm run lint`, `npm test` (77 passed), `npm run build`
all clean; only the pre-existing >500kB chunk warning.

