## The tax-year selector
## 2026-08-20

Third time this failure mode has shown up in this log: an iteration did the
whole task, left four modified files in the working tree, and died before
flipping the checkbox or committing. This iteration verified the work from
scratch — including re-checking every 2026 figure against the primary source —
made two small additions of its own, and committed.

### What it does

`TAX_YEAR_PARAMS: Record<TaxYear, TaxYearParams>` replaces `FILING_PARAMS`,
`LTCG_BRACKETS`, `ADDITIONAL_STD_DEDUCTION_65`, `MAX_ANNUAL_SS_BENEFIT` and
`AVG_ANNUAL_SS_BENEFIT`. `Scenario` gained a `year` field, so every call site
that already took a Scenario picked the year up for free — which is exactly the
payoff the previous iteration's refactor was for. Adding 2027 means adding one
entry and one member to `TAX_YEARS`; nothing downstream changes.

`federalIncomeTax(taxableIncome, scenario)` now takes the whole scenario rather
than a bare `filingStatus`, so a caller cannot pass a status and forget a year.

### The part that is the actual point

`SS_BASES` lives OUTSIDE `TAX_YEAR_PARAMS`, deliberately, and is commented to
say why. IRC 86(c) wrote $25,000/$32,000 in 1983 and $34,000/$44,000 in 1993 and
neither has ever been indexed. Everything else in the file is. Putting the
bases in the per-year record would have meant repeating them identically twice
and losing the contrast that the whole app exists to show.

The UI states it: half the benefit counts toward provisional income, so each
COLA eats the $25,000 base from the inside. Other income you can have before
*any* benefit is taxable, at each year's average retired-worker benefit:
$13,144 in 2025, $12,574 in 2026. Same real income, more tax, no vote.

On a separate return there is no headroom to erode — 86(c)(1)(C) zeroes both
bases outright — so that paragraph is swapped for one saying so rather than
rendering a meaningless number.

### Every 2026 figure, verified against the primary source

The Rev. Proc. 2025-32 PDF would not render (no poppler on this box) and
WebFetch got binary garbage out of it. Extracted the text instead by pulling
FlateDecode streams with python zlib and stripping whitespace — the PDF's
kerning splits digits ("20 25 - 32"), so searching only works on the flattened
string. Worth repeating next time a Rev. Proc. needs checking.

Confirmed verbatim from section 4:
- Rate tables: single/MFJ/MFS every threshold, including the MFS 35% band
  topping out at $384,350 while single still has room to $640,600.
- .14 Standard Deduction: $32,200 MFJ, $16,100 unmarried, $16,100 MFS.
- .14(3) Aged or blind: $1,650, raised to $2,050 if unmarried and not a
  surviving spouse. So single 2,050 / mfj 1,650 / mfs 1,650.
- .03 Maximum Capital Gains Rate: MFJ $98,900/$613,700, MFS $49,450/$306,850,
  All Other Individuals $49,450/$545,500.

SSA, separately: 2.8% COLA, $5,181/mo at age 70 ($62,172/yr), average retired
worker $2,071/mo in January 2026 ($24,852/yr).

Two things fell out of the numbers that are in code comments now:
- The OBBBA gave the bottom two brackets an extra inflation bump. Single 12%
  top went 48,475 -> 50,400 (+3.97%) while the 22% top went 103,350 -> 105,700
  (+2.27%). The 12% band widened by more than the bands above it.
- The 2025 MFS 15% LTCG band was $300,000 where half the joint band is
  $300,025 (separate rounding from separate base amounts). In 2026 it is
  $306,850, which IS exactly half of $613,700. The discrepancy is a
  rounding artifact, not a rule — do not "fix" either year to match the other.

### What this iteration added

- `defaultTaxYear` returned `calendarYear as TaxYear` for anything between the
  first and last year on file. That cast is only true while TAX_YEARS is
  contiguous; a future list of 2025 and 2027 would have handed 2026 back with
  no parameters behind it. Now it matches against the list and falls back to
  the last year. Test asserts the general invariant — every calendar year from
  first-5 to last+5 maps to a year that is actually on file.

### Clock pinning, which both test files needed

`defaultTaxYear()` reads `new Date()`, so an un-yeared scenario follows the wall
calendar. Every existing assertion is a 2025 dollar figure. Both test files now
`vi.useFakeTimers({ toFake: ['Date'] })` pinned to 2025-07-01 — Date only,
because faking setTimeout deadlocks React Testing Library and anything async.
One App test deliberately drops back to real timers: it checks that whatever
`defaultTaxYear()` returns *today* is a year the selector can render, so
shipping past the last year on file cannot open the app on a blank schedule.

