## 2026-08-20 — Task: Withdrawal sequencing comparison

Inherited a working tree from a crashed iteration that was already ~95% of the
way there: `src/utils/sequencing.ts` (665 lines), its 38-test suite, the App
section, and the CSS. It typechecked, linted, passed 290 tests and built. It
also had **zero App-level tests for the new section** — which is exactly the
hole the previous iteration's write-up warned about, and the reason a
`src/__probe.test.tsx` was sitting in the tree console-logging rendered prose:
that iteration was hand-eyeballing the branches when it died.

So this iteration was: audit the branches properly, fix what was wrong, and
pin every one of them with a test.

### What the model does (inherited, verified, unchanged)

Three orders — conventional (taxable → traditional → Roth), proportional, and
bracket filling (traditional up to a ceiling, then taxable, then Roth) — funding
the same retirement over the projection's horizon, COLA, birth year, IRA balance
and growth rate. Scored two ways: lifetime federal tax in first-year dollars,
and ending value net of the deferred bill on what is left. The second score
exists because the first is gameable by simply not withdrawing; the leftover IRA
is priced by running the whole balance through the final year's return rather
than at a marginal rate, since a filer who ends with no ordinary income has a
marginal rate of zero and that would value a seven-figure IRA at par.

The tax fixed point is the non-obvious part: tax is paid on top of spending, so
the withdrawal needed depends on the tax which depends on the withdrawal. It
iterates to a cent in ~5 passes (the marginal rate is the convergence factor)
with a 40-pass cap for the bracket-filling case that can oscillate, and then
recomputes the reported tax from the plan actually chosen so the row is
internally consistent even if the cap binds.

### Four prose defects, all found by probing branches the tests never reached

Same class as last iteration's: sentences asserting a *cause* nobody checked.

1. **The disagreement paragraph blamed an IRA that did not exist.** With the
   traditional balance at $0 the scores still disagree — proportional pays less
   tax but spends Roth dollars to do it — and the prose rendered "got there by
   leaving **$0** in the IRA with **$0** of tax still attached to it". True and
   an explanation of nothing. Now branched on `endingTraditional > 0`: the
   no-IRA arm names the actual bill, $406,835 of unrealised gain carrying
   $58,432 of tax, and the Roth gap ($262,859 against $397,995).
2. **"Already past the ceiling" was the catch-all for three different states.**
   The else-arm fired whenever bracket filling did not shrink the IRA, which
   covers (a) genuinely past the ceiling, (b) no IRA at all, (c) the spending
   drains the IRA under every order. With no IRA and $30,000 of income it
   claimed the filer was "already past the top of the 12% bracket", which is
   nowhere near true. Split three ways; the surviving "past the ceiling" arm now
   also names *what* breached it (other income + benefit + the RMD), since
   `fillRoom` includes the RMD and the old "before any withdrawal" did not.
3. **The tie paragraph misattributed a $0 spread.** At $150,000 of other income
   nothing is withdrawn by choice at all — income covers spending and its tax —
   yet the prose read "the sequencing decision is small next to how much is
   being withdrawn" and advised *less* spending, which makes the tie tighter.
   Two new arms ahead of it: nothing-to-sequence (no voluntary withdrawal) and
   only-one-funded-account. The genuine close-race arm still fires and is tested
   ($268 spread at $30k income / $50k spending / $25k IRA).
4. **The tooltip quoted one order's RMD as if it were everyone's.** The header
   showed `strategies[0].rmd`. By 2044 on a $1M IRA the conventional order is
   required to take $82,730 and bracket filling $53,210 — the header figure was
   wrong for two of the three lines it sat above. Moved onto each strategy's own
   line.

Also replaced `sequencing.strategies[0]` / `[2]` with lookups by id. That
positional indexing is the same shape as the bug commit eceb968 was written to
kill, and reordering `SEQUENCING_STRATEGIES` for presentation would have
silently swapped which order the prose compared.

### One field added

`StrategyProjection.voluntaryWithdrawal` — everything withdrawn by choice, i.e.
the whole taxable and Roth draw plus traditional above the RMD. Zero is the
clean signal for "the orders never had a decision to make", which is what
defect 3 needed and could not be read off the existing fields.

### Tests

16 in `App.test.tsx` walking every branch (both winner arms, both disagreement
arms, all four bracket-fill arms, all three tie arms, the shortfall arm, the
year re-date, the basis-cents readout, the absent IRMAA ceiling) plus 2 for the
tooltip. 3 in `sequencing.test.ts` for `voluntaryWithdrawal`. 308 total, up
from 290 — 286 after deleting the inherited probe file.

Note for whoever writes the next branchy section: the engine suite passing 38
tests told me nothing about whether the section on screen said true things. The
probe-render-and-read-the-text loop is what found all four, and it takes about
five minutes. Do that before writing the assertions, not instead of them.

