## 2026-08-20 — Task: QCD modeling

Inherited another near-complete working tree from a crashed iteration:
`Scenario.qcd` threaded through tax.ts, the `qcdEffect` comparison, the slider,
the section, the CSS accent, 15 engine tests and 13 App tests. It typechecked,
linted, passed 336 tests and built. Verified the statutory figures against IRS
Notice 2024-80 ($108,000 / $54,000 for 2025) and Notice 2025-67 ($111,000 /
$55,000 for 2026) — both right, including the per-individual doubling on a
joint return and the 70½ floor that neither SECURE act moved.

So this iteration was the same job as the last two: probe the prose branches the
tests never reached, and pin what survives.

### What the model does (inherited, verified, unchanged)

A QCD is taken *out of* `ordinaryIncome` rather than added on top of it — the
gift is a distribution that would have been reported anyway — and capped twice:
by the annual limit, then by the ordinary income there is to exclude it from,
because 408(d)(8) excludes a distribution and there has to be a distribution.
Everything downstream reads `ordinaryIncomeAfterQcd`, so the exclusion never
reaches AGI, never reaches provisional income under 86(b)(2), and never reaches
Medicare's MAGI two years later. The section prices it against the same gift
taken as an ordinary distribution, which is the choice a donor actually faces —
not against not giving.

### Five prose defects, all found by rendering the section and reading it

Same class as the last two iterations': sentences asserting a *cause* nobody
checked.

1. **"Provisional income stays under $25,000 either way" with $100,000 of
   provisional income.** The no-benefits-move arm fell through to the
   under-the-base explanation whenever `taxableSSWithout === 0`, which is also
   true when the benefit itself is $0. Now branched on `ssBenefit === 0`.
2. **Every flat taxable-SS line was blamed on "the 85% cap".** Section 86 has
   two ceilings — half the benefit in the first tier, 85% in the second — and
   flat is the only symptom either shows. With a $6,000 benefit and $30,000 of
   income the gift moves provisional income $33,000 → $31,000 and $3,000 stays
   taxable: that is the *50%* cap, and the 85% tier ($34,000) is never in play.
   New `ssCapPercent` reads `taxableSSWith` against `0.85 * ssBenefit` to tell
   them apart, and each arm now names what the cap actually is.
3. **"The excluded dollars were not carrying any federal tax" when nothing was
   excluded.** With $0 of ordinary income and $100,000 of gains the exclusion is
   capped to zero, so the scenario is identical either way — and the section
   explained a saving of $0 by describing dollars that did not exist. New
   `excluded === 0` arm ahead of it, which names the real obstacle and, when
   there is tax, says whose it is: $10,185 on the $125,500 of gains and taxable
   benefit left in the base, none of it reachable by a QCD.
4. **"The $15,750 of deductions already covered the whole return" against
   $45,000 of AGI.** The zero-tax arm assumed the deduction was the reason.
   With $5,000 of income and $40,000 of gains the deduction covers $15,750 of
   it; what zeroes the bill is $29,250 of taxable income sitting under the
   $48,350 top of the 0% gains bracket. Split on `agiWithout <= deductionTotal`.
5. **"The next dollar is worth 0% in federal tax" as the opening pitch at $0 of
   income.** Arithmetically right, and a reader takes it as a verdict on QCDs
   rather than on the empty scenario. Now prompts for income instead.

Also branched the "more gift than distribution" warning, which read "There is
only $0 of ordinary income ... so that is all the chart can take out".

### Tests

9 new in `App.test.tsx` (both cap arms, the no-benefit arm, the under-the-base
arm, both zero-tax arms, the nothing-excluded arm with and without tax, the
empty-scenario prompt), 2 rewritten where the prose changed. 342 total, up from
336. Every arm of the section is now pinned, including the negative assertions
that keep the wrong cause from creeping back — `not.toHaveTextContent(/85%
cap/)` is what would have caught defect 2 the first time.

The probe-render-and-read-the-text loop found all five again, in about ten
minutes. Third iteration running: the engine suite passing tells you nothing
about whether the section on screen says true things.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (342 passed), npm run build — all
clean; only the pre-existing >500kB chunk warning.

## 2026-08-20 — Task: Head of Household filing status

Clean tree this time, so the whole iteration was the task. Added `'hoh'` to
`FilingStatus` and filled in everything the exhaustive `Record<FilingStatus, …>`
types demanded, which is the reason those types are there — tsc named all six
sites before I touched the UI.

### The blocker in Discovered Work was based on a wrong premise

The plan said to rework `.tier-table` before adding HOH, because "the IRMAA
table has grown a column per status — a fourth will not fit". It does not need
a fourth. 42 U.S.C. 1395r(i)(3)(C) has exactly three clauses: (ii) a joint
return, (iii) a married individual filing separately, and (i) "an individual …
who is not described in clause (ii) or (iii)". A head of household is not
described in either, so it lands in (i) with single filers, and SSA's POMS HI
01101.020 heads that table "Single, head-of-household, or qualifying surviving
spouse with dependent child tax filing status". So `magiOver.hoh` is the single
column repeated, the table stayed at six columns, and the only change was
relabelling "MAGI (single)" to "MAGI (individual)" with a caption sentence
saying why there are three tables and not four. Worth checking the statute
before doing the rework a plan bullet asks for.

### Figures, and where each came from

Verified against Rev. Proc. 2024-40 / 2025-32 (via Tax Foundation's tables and
currentfederaltaxdevelopments' Rev. Proc. walkthrough, since the IRS PDF does
not extract) rather than recalled:

| | 2025 | 2026 |
|---|---|---|
| standard deduction | $23,625 (OBBBA, not the $22,500 the Rev. Proc. printed) | $24,150 |
| age-65 addition | $2,000 | $2,050 |
| 10% / 12% tops | $17,000 / $64,850 | $17,700 / $67,450 |
| 22%–37% tops | 103,350 / 197,300 / 250,500 / 626,350 | 105,700 / 201,775 / 256,200 / 640,600 |
| 0% / 15% gain tops | $64,750 / $566,700 | $66,200 / $579,600 |

Two traps: the standard deduction is 1.5x the single one *after* the OBBBA
rewrote 63(c)(2) for 2025, and the 65+ addition is the unmarried $2,000/$2,050
under 63(f)(3), not the married $1,600/$1,650 — one source quoted "$1,650" for
HOH because it read only the first sentence of the Rev. Proc. paragraph, which
gives the base amount before the unmarried uplift.

Everything else fell out of statute that already keys on "joint or not":
`maxSeniors` 1, the QCD limit undoubled, `SENIOR_DEDUCTION_PHASEOUT_START`
$75,000 (151(d)(5)(C)(i) says $150,000 for a joint return and $75,000
otherwise; clause (v) only bars separate filers), and SS bases $25,000/$34,000
under 86(c)(1)'s "any other case".

### The one genuinely interesting number

Head of household keeps a single filer's thresholds exactly and changes
everything downstream — so the torpedo *starts* at the same provisional income
and the whole curve still shifts right, because the bigger deduction takes
longer to fill. The shift is not the deduction gap. Inside the 50% tier each
extra dollar of other income raises AGI by $1.50 (the dollar plus fifty cents
of dragged-in benefit), so $7,875 of extra deduction buys $5,250 of income:
first taxed dollar at $14,882 single, $20,132 head of household. That ratio is
now an assertion rather than a coincidence.

### One prose defect found by probing, and it was a stale constant

Rendering the *separate*-return note (not the new one) turned up "The brackets
and standard deduction are identical up to $375,800 of taxable income" written
as a literal. That figure is the top of the separate 35% band — where 1(j)(2)(D)
halving stops matching the single schedule — and it is indexed: $384,350 in
2026. It has been silently wrong for the 2026 selector since the year task
landed. Now derived from `filingParams(year, 'mfs').brackets`, pinned in both
years, and logged under Discovered work.

### Tests

12 engine tests in `tax.test.ts` (Pub 915 agreement at the default base amounts,
threshold identity with single across a sweep, the 1.5x deduction relation over
both years, the unmarried 65+ addition, both bracket schedules, both gain
schedules, the $1.50 first-taxed-dollar relation, the senior deduction at the
unmarried threshold, per-person figures, the shared Medicare column, the
conversion ceilings). 11 App tests covering the new status note, its re-dating
to 2026, its absence under the other three statuses, and the divergence-figure
fix. The Pub 915 reference worksheet got `hoh` keys of its own rather than
reading `SS_BASES`, so it stays an independent implementation. 365 total, up
from 342.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (365 passed), npm run build — all
