## Lump-sum election for retroactive SS awards (IRC 86(e))

Picked up an iteration that had already written the feature and then stopped
before running anything. ~2,000 uncommitted lines across a new `lumpSum.ts`,
its tests, a UI section and a 26-line change to `tax.ts`. Nothing had been
typechecked, linted, built or run. This iteration finished it: four defects,
three of them real.

### What the previous iteration got right, and it is the load-bearing part

`taxableSSCap` on `Scenario`, applied as a `Math.min` at the end of
`taxableSocialSecurity`. 86(e)(1) caps *gross income*, not tax — so the earlier
years contribute an inclusion and the year of receipt taxes it at its own
rates. Because the cap lands inside the one function everything else reads
through, AGI, the senior deduction's 6% phaseout, the gain stacking and
Medicare's MAGI two years out all follow it without knowing it exists. No
call site outside `lumpSumElection` sets it and none needed changing.

### Four defects

1. `.at(-1)` in a test against the project's ES2020 `lib` — tsc error. Fixed the
   one line rather than widening `lib`; it was the only `.at(` in `src/`.
2. Two DOM assertions written with a curly apostrophe (`year’s`) where App.tsx
   emits `&apos;`, which renders straight. Repo convention, already visible in
   the committed tests: curly in `it(...)` names, straight in assertions.
3. **The knife-edge test asserted a figure the scenario does not produce.** At
   $60,000 of other income the no-election figure is $57,200, not the $61,200
   its own comment calls "85% here" — provisional income of $96,000 leaves it a
   tier-2 slice, $4,000 short of the ceiling. The engine was right. The contrast
   the test is built on (aggregate at 85%, per-year not) only exists above
   $64,706 of other income, so the scenario moved to $65,000 rather than the
   expectation moving to $57,200 — otherwise the test keeps its name and loses
   its point.
4. **A statutory claim that contradicted itself two paragraphs later.** The
   "do not elect" branch said the election "is irrevocable once made"; the field
   note below said 86(e)(2)(B) makes it "revocable only with the consent of the
   Secretary". Both were asserted by tests, so the suite was pinning a
   contradiction. Checked the statute rather than picking the nicer sentence:
   26 U.S.C. 86(e)(2)(B) reads "Such election, once made, may be revoked only
   with the consent of the Secretary." The field note was right; the branch is
   now derived from the same wording.

### The thing worth knowing about this feature

The election runs entirely on frozen numbers. 86(c) has never been indexed, so a
2019 back-pay year is figured against the same $25,000 and $34,000 as a 2025
one — which is why the worksheet can reach back into years the app has no
brackets, no standard deduction and no benefit figures for. It needs none of
them: no rate schedule is ever consulted for a prior year, because 86(e)(1) caps
an amount of income and not an amount of tax. That is also why the section is
the only one on the page immune to the year selector.

Consequence for the default scenario: 24 months of back pay at the average
benefit saves $13,299 of taxable benefit and $1,595 of federal tax, because two
waiting years hand back two unused sets of thresholds while the no-election
figure is already deep in the 85% tier. Past the point where every waiting year
is *itself* in the 85% tier the two lines climb in parallel — the election locks
in what it saved rather than saving more, which is the shape the chart shows.

`capBindsEveryYear` deserves its name: it is a claim about each year's own
benefit, and reading it off the aggregate would be wrong in both directions.
Defect 3 was exactly that mistake made by the test author.

### Verification

npx tsc -b, npm run lint, npx vitest run (417 passed, up from 365),
npm run build — all clean; only the pre-existing >500kB chunk warning.
